  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal system passes through the region \nlater today and tonight producing widespread showers with \nlocalized flash flooding.\n\nHigh pressure over the area moves offshore during the early\nmorning hours. Low pressure over the Midwest tracks east. A warm\nfront ahead of that low lifts north through the region this\nafternoon, and then this will be followed by low pressure \npassing through the region late tonight.\n\nSeveral waves of showers and scattered thunderstorms will \nimpact the area this afternoon and tonight. The first wave of \nshowers will develop as the warm front lifts north through the \nregion. Most rainfall associated with this front will average\n1/4 to 1/2 inch.\n\nBehind the warm front, moisture increases over the area,\nresulting in surface dew points rising into the low 70s and\nPWATs of 2 to 2.25 inches. With low pressure passing through the\nregion tonight, showers and scattered thunderstorms develop with\nlocally heavy rain. While localized flash flooding is possible\ndue to localized amounts of 1 to 2 inches of rain, most showers\nwill be fairly progressive, so will not issue a Flash Flood\nWatch at this time. The Weather Prediction Center has a Marginal\nRisk for Excessive Rainfall, which is a 1 out of 4. The Slight\nrisk has been cut back to portions to southeast Pennsylvania\nwest of Philadelphia.\n\nThe severe threat is fairly minimal, generally focused on\nDelmarva where the Storm Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk\n(1 out of 5) for Delmarva and extreme southern New Jersey.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A trailing mid-level short wave trough and weak\ncold front lead to the potential for additional rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms on Monday.\n\nThe cold front associated with the low that approaches the \nregion on Sunday will finally progress through the region and \nmove offshore on Monday. Additionally, in the the mid-levels, a \ntrailing short wave trough will cross overhead. The net result \nis that isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will be \npossible during the day on Monday. \n\nAs for hazards, this round appears to have very limited threats\nfor both flash flooding and severe weather. Convection on \nMonday will be much more weakly forced (as compared to \nSunday/Sunday night), and bulk shear values are depicted in the \n20 to 25 kt range, which is rather modest. Although there will \nbe instability present, the modest shear will limit storm \norganization. Model soundings still depict relatively high \nprecipitable water values of upwards of 2 inches, and warm cloud\nlayers above 10K ft, which results in some heavy rain threat. \nHowever, with limited shear and coverage of convection, the \nthreat for any one area to get heavy rain for long enough to \nresult in flash flooding remains low.\n\nFor those hoping for a significant cool down in the wake of \nthis cold front, unfortunately the temperature gradient is very \nsmall. As such highs on Tuesday will be at or just a degree or \ntwo lower than Monday. That being said, the front should usher \nin drier air, so regardless it should feel more comfortable on \nTuesday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front is expected to cross through the \nregion Wednesday night into Thursday resulting in more shower \nand thunderstorm chances.\n\nThe next main focus is another cold front arriving in the \nregion during the Wednesday night into Thursday time period. \nThere are some big timing differences between models on when \nexactly the front will arrive and when the mid-level trough will\ncross offshore. As such, there is considerable uncertainty with\nthe potential hazards as the severe and hydro threats will be \ndependent on the prime time for any showers and thunderstorms.\n\nIn terms of temperatures, so far this appears to be a stronger \ncold front compared to the one earlier in the week. As such, \ntemperatures by the end of the week and into the weekend are \ncurrently forecast to be around 5 degrees lower compared to the \nmidweek time period.",
